{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And when an ox gores a man.</b> Up to this point, damages done by a person; from here on, damages done by other creatures to a person." "<b>And when an ox gores a man.</b> Goring (נגיחה) applies to a person, and goring/butting (נגיפה) to an animal, because a person has a guardian angel (מזל) and the ox does not kill him except by goring him; but an animal has no guardian angel, and there is no fear upon its fellow, and it kills." "<b>It shall surely be stoned, the ox.</b> And if you ask: if there, in the case of stoning, it is already forewarned (מועד), all the more so — 'the ox shall be stoned,' why do I need it? Rather, no — if you say this about a tam ox (one not previously forewarned), that it is by stoning, that is because it does not pay ransom (כופר); you might say the same about a muad ox, which does pay ransom — therefore Scripture says: 'the ox shall be stoned.' And if you ask: since a tam ox is killed when it kills a person, how can it become forewarned for two or three times? One may answer: for example, it escaped into the marsh after the first gorings; or alternatively, they recognize the owner of the ox but do not recognize the ox itself, and they say to him: 'you have a goring ox among your cattle,' as is stated in Bava Kamma." "<b>And its flesh shall not be eaten.</b> From the implication of what is stated, 'it shall surely be stoned,' I do not yet know that it may not be eaten; rather, it tells you: even though an ordinary nevelah (carcass) is permitted to dogs, this one is forbidden." "<b>And the owner of the ox is clean.</b> As long as it had not been forewarned (הועד)."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:35.702972185Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}